11:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L2b",null,{"guide":{"date":"September 17–22, 1862","place":"The southern and northern ends of the Antietam battlefield around Sharpsburg, Maryland; field hospitals across the Antietam Valley; and the roads through Middletown to Frederick, Maryland, and across the Potomac at Boteler’s Ford near Shepherdstown, Virginia","result":"A. P. Hill’s Light Division struck the exposed left flank of the Ninth Corps late on September 17. The 8th and 16th Connecticut, 4th Rhode Island, and supporting Ohio regiments were driven back, Isaac Rodman was mortally wounded, and Lawrence O’Bryan Branch was killed. Jacob Cox withdrew the Ninth Corps to defensible ground above the Lower Bridge, which remained in Federal hands. On the Confederate left, concentrated Federal artillery stopped Jackson and Stuart from turning the Federal right. Fighting ended near sunset. Neither army renewed the battle on September 18; Lee withdrew across the Potomac that night. Nearly 23,000 soldiers were casualties, and the Union strategic victory gave Lincoln the opportunity to announce the Preliminary Emancipation Proclamation on September 22","facts":[{"label":"Episode scope","value":"The final fighting on September 17 and the immediate aftermath through September 20","note":"The episode begins with A. P. Hill’s counterattack, follows the close of combat and medical response, and ends with the Confederate withdrawal, limited Federal pursuit, and the battle’s strategic consequences."},{"label":"Hill’s forced march","value":"About 15–17 miles from Harpers Ferry","note":"NPS interpretation describes roughly 3,500 men leaving Harpers Ferry at 7:00 a.m.; three brigades, about 2,500 men, reached the decisive sector in time to attack. The episode estimates about 2,000 initially available after straggling and trailing brigades reduced the first wave."},{"label":"Hill’s tactical advantage","value":"Position, surprise, and veteran troops","note":"The Light Division entered south of the Ninth Corps and struck its open left flank rather than attacking the Federal line frontally."},{"label":"Exposed Federal flank","value":"Harland’s 8th and 16th Connecticut and 4th Rhode Island","note":"These regiments formed the left of Rodman’s division and the entire Army of the Potomac. Difficult terrain and uneven movement separated the 8th Connecticut from the other two regiments."},{"label":"16th Connecticut experience","value":"Less than one month in Federal service","note":"The regiment mustered on August 24, reached Washington on September 1, and joined the Army of the Potomac only days before fighting at Antietam."},{"label":"Rodman mortally wounded","value":"Shot through the chest while reacting to Hill’s arrival","note":"Brig. Gen. Isaac P. Rodman was carried from the field and died on September 30, 1862."},{"label":"8th Connecticut advance","value":"Several hundred yards ahead of the rest of Harland’s brigade","note":"The regiment advanced with Fairchild’s brigade, captured a Confederate battery near the Harpers Ferry Road, and then lost about half its men when attacked in flank."},{"label":"Otto’s 40-acre cornfield","value":"Head-high corn and broken ground concealed the attackers","note":"The 16th Connecticut and 4th Rhode Island could not see Gregg’s South Carolinians approaching from the south and west. Smoke, crops, and some Confederate clothing taken at Harpers Ferry added to the identification problem."},{"label":"Gregg’s attack","value":"Surprise fire from two directions","note":"Maxcy Gregg’s veteran South Carolinians struck the inexperienced 16th Connecticut and the 4th Rhode Island in and around the cornfield."},{"label":"Federal left collapses","value":"About 4:30 p.m. in the episode","note":"The 16th Connecticut routed, the 4th Rhode Island withdrew, and the retreat exposed the supporting Ohio regiments and troops farther north."},{"label":"Cox orders a withdrawal","value":"The Ninth Corps gives up its gains near Sharpsburg","note":"Cox pulled back the troops near town because Hill’s attack had broken the corps left and threatened to separate or envelop the forward formations."},{"label":"Three Confederate brigades engaged","value":"Gregg, Archer, and Branch","note":"NPS interpretation places Gregg against the 16th Connecticut and 4th Rhode Island, Archer between Gregg and Branch, and Branch moving north against the 8th Connecticut and Fairchild."},{"label":"Ohio support line","value":"Scammon’s Kanawha brigade changes front","note":"The 12th, 23rd, and 30th Ohio confronted Hill’s attack. They helped prevent a complete collapse but could not restore the advance toward Sharpsburg."},{"label":"Lawrence O’Bryan Branch killed","value":"Shot in the head while leading his North Carolina brigade","note":"Branch died during the final Confederate counterattack and was one of six general officers killed or mortally wounded at Antietam."},{"label":"Six general officers lost","value":"Three United States and three Confederate","note":"Joseph K. F. Mansfield, Israel B. Richardson, and Isaac P. Rodman were the Federal fatalities; William E. Starke, George B. Anderson, and Lawrence O’Bryan Branch were the Confederate fatalities."},{"label":"Confederate artillery at the final attack","value":"More than forty guns in the episode","note":"Lee and his officers concentrated pieces from numerous batteries south of Sharpsburg. Their crossfire supported Hill and helped force Federal batteries west of the creek to withdraw."},{"label":"Ninth Corps final position","value":"Curved ridge above the Lower Bridge","note":"Cox’s line held the west-bank bridgehead. Sturgis and Fairchild were on the left, Scammon in the center, and Willcox on the right."},{"label":"Jackson’s northern counterattack plan","value":"Turn the Federal right between the army and the Potomac","note":"Jackson assigned Stuart a small mixed force of cavalry, artillery, and infantry. The movement was abandoned when the strength of the Federal artillery and the extension of the Federal line made the route untenable."},{"label":"Federal artillery on the right","value":"A concentrated line near the Joseph Poffenberger farm","note":"Federal batteries under the I Corps sector suppressed the Confederate guns in about fifteen minutes in the episode’s account and stopped Stuart’s movement before it developed."},{"label":"Unused Federal reserve","value":"Most of Fitz John Porter’s V Corps remained uncommitted","note":"McClellan and Porter retained the infantry near headquarters because they regarded it as the army’s only reliable reserve while both flanks appeared threatened."},{"label":"McClellan’s order to Burnside","value":"Hold the ground and especially the bridge","note":"A courier reported that the Ninth Corps needed men and guns. McClellan sent Miller’s battery but no infantry reinforcement."},{"label":"Close of combat","value":"Firing declined near sunset, about 5:30 p.m.","note":"The Ninth Corps retained the Lower Bridge and a west-bank foothold; Hill’s tired brigades did not attempt to drive the corps across Antietam Creek."},{"label":"Battle casualties","value":"Nearly 23,000 killed, wounded, or missing","note":"Exact totals vary. Antietam National Battlefield uses an overall estimate of 22,720 and about 3,200 casualties in the final-attack sector."},{"label":"Deaths after the battle","value":"Many wounded soldiers died later","note":"The episode estimates that 6,300–6,500 men were killed outright or died of wounds. This combines immediate deaths with later mortality and is distinct from the total casualty count."},{"label":"Letterman ambulance system","value":"Organized transport and trained personnel","note":"The Army of the Potomac’s new ambulance corps assigned vehicles and trained crews to collect wounded men and move them through aid stations, field hospitals, and permanent hospitals."},{"label":"Field hospitals","value":"At least 71 Federal hospitals in the episode","note":"Houses, churches, barns, and sheds across the Antietam Valley held wounded soldiers. NPS educational material describes the Smith barn as one of about seventy-five field hospitals in the area."},{"label":"Medical supply shortage","value":"Army supplies arrived several days after the battle","note":"Rail damage, transportation priorities, and the speed of the campaign delayed official supplies. Surgeons relied on what they carried and what civilians and relief organizations supplied."},{"label":"Clara Barton","value":"Brought bandages, food, and lanterns to the northern battlefield","note":"Barton arrived near the Poffenberger farm around noon on September 17 and worked with wounded soldiers and surgeons through the battle."},{"label":"U.S. Sanitary Commission","value":"Independent transportation brought relief supplies on September 18","note":"The civilian commission’s wagons helped bridge the interval before the Army Medical Department’s larger supply shipments reached the hospitals."},{"label":"Evacuation to Frederick","value":"About twenty miles through Middletown","note":"Beginning September 20, ambulances and walking wounded moved east across South Mountain. By September 25, about 600 soldiers considered too badly wounded to travel remained in two battlefield tent hospitals."},{"label":"Frederick hospital network","value":"The existing barracks plus six temporary hospitals","note":"Churches, public buildings, and tents expanded capacity. More than half the patients were eventually transferred to Washington, Baltimore, or Philadelphia."},{"label":"Confederate wounded left behind","value":"About 2,000 in the episode","note":"Lee’s army evacuated those it could but left the most severely wounded in Maryland and near Shepherdstown, sometimes with Confederate surgeons who remained to care for them."},{"label":"September 18","value":"Neither army renewed the battle","note":"Franklin proposed another attack on the Confederate left, but McClellan declined. Lee began withdrawing across the Potomac after dark."},{"label":"September 19–20","value":"Limited pursuit followed by the Battle of Shepherdstown","note":"V Corps troops crossed the Potomac on September 19. A larger crossing on September 20 was repulsed by A. P. Hill’s division."},{"label":"Strategic result","value":"Lee’s Maryland invasion ended","note":"The battlefield result was tactically inconclusive, but Lee’s withdrawal allowed the United States to claim a strategic victory."},{"label":"Emancipation consequence","value":"Preliminary Emancipation Proclamation announced September 22","note":"Lincoln used the end of Lee’s invasion as the military result he had awaited before announcing the proclamation."},{"label":"Federal force utilization","value":"About one-third of McClellan’s available army did not fire a shot in the episode’s estimate","note":"The episode contrasts the heavy losses in I, II, XII, and IX Corps with the limited engagement of V and VI Corps formations."},{"label":"Recommended books","value":"Two Gary W. Gallagher essay collections","note":"The episode recommends “Antietam: Essays on the 1862 Maryland Campaign” and “The Antietam Campaign.”"},{"label":"Calixa Lavallée","value":"4th Rhode Island musician and later composer of the music for “O Canada”","note":"The bonus segment identifies the Quebec-born soldier as wounded during Hill’s counterattack. His surname is properly spelled Lavallée."},{"label":"Transcript normalizations","value":"Harland, Maxcy Gregg, Kanawha, Poffenberger, Pleasonton, Pry House, and Calixa Lavallée","note":"The automated transcript produces forms including “Harlan,” “Maxi Gregg,” “Canaw,” “Paffenberger,” “Pleasanton,” “pry house,” and “Kai Douglas.” This guide uses the historical forms; “Kai Douglas” is normalized to Henry Kyd Douglas."}],"maps":[{"title":"A. P. Hill strikes the Ninth Corps left","caption":"Harland’s 8th Connecticut had moved ahead toward the Harpers Ferry Road, leaving the 16th Connecticut and 4th Rhode Island in Otto’s 40-acre cornfield. Gregg attacked from the south, Archer moved between Gregg and Branch, and Branch drove north against the 8th Connecticut and Fairchild. Scammon’s Ohio regiments changed front as Cox withdrew the Ninth Corps toward the Lower Bridge. Sequential actions are combined, positions are generalized, and the map is not to scale.","source":"Antietam National Battlefield Final Attack Trail, Cox’s and Harland’s official reports, Antietam Battlefield Board atlas, and episode transcript; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep199-hill-counterattack.svg","alt":"Schematic map of A. P. Hill’s brigades attacking the exposed Ninth Corps left around Otto’s 40-acre cornfield and the Harpers Ferry Road."},{"title":"Close of battle and immediate aftermath","caption":"The Ninth Corps held the Lower Bridge while Federal artillery near the Joseph Poffenberger farm stopped the proposed Confederate movement around the Federal right. On September 18 Lee remained near Sharpsburg, then withdrew through Shepherdstown after dark. Wounded soldiers moved from battlefield hospitals through Middletown to Frederick beginning September 20. This context map covers different times and is schematic, with routes and positions generalized.","source":"Antietam National Battlefield timeline and medical interpretation, Jackson’s official report, and episode transcript; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep199-close-and-aftermath.svg","alt":"Context map showing the final positions at Antietam, the Confederate withdrawal through Shepherdstown, and the Federal medical evacuation route to Frederick."},{"title":"Smith’s barn field hospital","caption":"Alexander Gardner photographed the Smith house and barn near Keedysville in September 1862. The property was used as a hospital after Antietam, one of dozens of improvised treatment sites across the valley.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep199-smith-hospital-barn.jpg","alt":"Alexander Gardner photograph of the Smith house and barn near Keedysville, used as a field hospital after Antietam."}],"moments":[{"time":"1:09","audioTime":69,"title":"The Ninth Corps crisis is recapped","detail":"The corps has reached the outskirts of Sharpsburg, but its exposed left threatens the entire advance."},{"time":"1:25","audioTime":85,"title":"A. P. Hill arrives from Harpers Ferry","detail":"Hill’s Light Division reaches the battlefield at the point where it can strike the open Federal flank."},{"time":"1:46","audioTime":106,"title":"The forced march reduces Hill’s strength","detail":"The episode estimates that about 2,000 of the roughly 3,300 men who left Harpers Ferry are available at the first crisis."},{"time":"2:16","audioTime":136,"title":"Hill angles toward the Federal left","detail":"The position and surprise of the Confederate arrival multiply the effect of the veteran troops."},{"time":"2:25","audioTime":145,"title":"Harland’s regiments hold the flank","detail":"The 8th and 16th Connecticut and 4th Rhode Island form the vulnerable edge of Rodman’s division."},{"time":"2:47","audioTime":167,"title":"The 16th Connecticut’s inexperience is explained","detail":"The regiment had been in Federal service for less than a month and had almost no opportunity for field training."},{"time":"3:04","audioTime":184,"title":"Rodman sees the threat","detail":"He identifies Hill’s column moving toward his division from the south and rides forward to warn the line."},{"time":"3:11","audioTime":191,"title":"Rodman is mortally wounded","detail":"A Confederate bullet strikes the Ninth Corps division commander in the chest."},{"time":"3:25","audioTime":205,"title":"Harland loses his horse","detail":"The brigade commander’s horse is shot while he tries to reach the two trailing regiments."},{"time":"3:39","audioTime":219,"title":"The 8th Connecticut advances alone","detail":"A delay separates the regiment by several hundred yards from the 16th Connecticut and 4th Rhode Island."},{"time":"3:51","audioTime":231,"title":"The 8th Connecticut captures a battery","detail":"The regiment reaches Confederate guns near the Harpers Ferry Road before Hill’s counterattack strikes its flank."},{"time":"4:11","audioTime":251,"title":"Two regiments enter Otto’s cornfield","detail":"The 4th Rhode Island and 16th Connecticut move into head-high corn on broken ground half a mile behind the 8th Connecticut."},{"time":"4:29","audioTime":269,"title":"Gregg approaches from the south and west","detail":"The South Carolina brigade uses the concealed approach to reach the inexperienced Federal regiment."},{"time":"4:43","audioTime":283,"title":"Crossfire surprises the Federals","detail":"Gregg’s troops fire from two directions into the 16th Connecticut and then the 4th Rhode Island."},{"time":"5:06","audioTime":306,"title":"The 16th Connecticut collapses","detail":"Inexperienced officers and soldiers cannot execute the needed formation changes under close fire."},{"time":"5:43","audioTime":343,"title":"Clothing adds to the confusion","detail":"Some Confederates wear pieces of captured Federal clothing, making identification harder in the corn and smoke."},{"time":"6:02","audioTime":362,"title":"The Federal flank routs","detail":"The 16th Connecticut breaks and carries the 4th Rhode Island back with it."},{"time":"6:26","audioTime":386,"title":"The forward Ninth Corps line is endangered","detail":"Hill’s attack threatens troops that had advanced close to Sharpsburg and were unaware of the collapse to their left."},{"time":"6:36","audioTime":396,"title":"Cox orders the forward troops back","detail":"The corps commander gives up ground near Sharpsburg to prevent the advance from being cut off."},{"time":"7:02","audioTime":422,"title":"Three of Hill’s brigades are engaged","detail":"Gregg, Archer, and Branch continue the attack into the Ninth Corps left and supports."},{"time":"7:12","audioTime":432,"title":"Scammon’s Ohioans contest the cornfield","detail":"The supporting Kanawha brigade changes front but is outflanked and forced to withdraw."},{"time":"7:28","audioTime":448,"title":"Lawrence O’Bryan Branch is killed","detail":"The North Carolina brigade commander is shot in the head while riding forward and dies immediately."},{"time":"7:45","audioTime":465,"title":"The six general-officer fatalities are listed","detail":"The episode identifies three Federal and three Confederate generals killed or mortally wounded at Antietam."},{"time":"8:18","audioTime":498,"title":"Confederate guns concentrate south of town","detail":"More than forty field pieces in the episode support Hill and the remnants of D. R. Jones’s command."},{"time":"8:54","audioTime":534,"title":"Federal batteries west of the creek withdraw","detail":"The concentrated Confederate artillery outguns the Federal pieces that had crossed near the Middle Bridge."},{"time":"9:13","audioTime":553,"title":"Cox assembles a defensive line","detail":"The Ninth Corps falls back to preserve its west-bank foothold and control of the Lower Bridge."},{"time":"9:28","audioTime":568,"title":"McClellan watches the southern crisis","detail":"From the Pry House, the army commander observes the reversal on Burnside’s front."},{"time":"9:41","audioTime":581,"title":"New cannon fire erupts on the Federal right","detail":"The noise marks the opening of a separate Confederate effort on the northern end of the field."},{"time":"10:10","audioTime":610,"title":"Jackson’s counterattack plan is explained","detail":"Lee had asked Jackson to turn the Federal right and relieve pressure elsewhere on the Confederate line."},{"time":"10:34","audioTime":634,"title":"Stuart receives a mixed force","detail":"One infantry regiment, several batteries, and cavalry are assigned to move between the Federal flank and the Potomac."},{"time":"11:07","audioTime":667,"title":"Meade’s artillery blocks the route","detail":"Federal guns near the Joseph Poffenberger farm command the ground Stuart must cross."},{"time":"11:26","audioTime":686,"title":"Confederate batteries are suppressed","detail":"The Federal gun line silences the opposing artillery in about fifteen minutes in the episode’s account."},{"time":"11:41","audioTime":701,"title":"Stuart cancels the movement","detail":"The proposed turn of the Federal right ends before the Confederate infantry can advance."},{"time":"12:12","audioTime":732,"title":"George Smalley observes headquarters","detail":"The correspondent records McClellan watching the Ninth Corps and considering the V Corps reserve."},{"time":"12:35","audioTime":755,"title":"Porter’s infantry remains in reserve","detail":"McClellan and Porter decide that the V Corps troops cannot be spared while both flanks appear threatened."},{"time":"13:13","audioTime":793,"title":"Pleasonton’s request is recalled","detail":"Headquarters had already refused to release Porter’s infantry for an advance from the Middle Bridge."},{"time":"13:50","audioTime":830,"title":"McClellan and Porter ride toward the left","detail":"They leave the Pry House to inspect the known crisis on the Ninth Corps front."},{"time":"14:09","audioTime":849,"title":"Burnside asks for reinforcements","detail":"A courier reports that the Ninth Corps needs infantry and artillery to hold west of the creek."},{"time":"14:21","audioTime":861,"title":"McClellan orders the bridge held","detail":"He sends Miller’s battery but tells the courier that no infantry is available."},{"time":"15:21","audioTime":921,"title":"The Ninth Corps keeps its foothold","detail":"Hill’s exhausted men do not drive the Federals back across Antietam Creek or recapture the Lower Bridge."},{"time":"15:34","audioTime":934,"title":"The northern front remains static","detail":"Stuart’s abandoned movement leaves both armies holding their existing positions."},{"time":"15:43","audioTime":943,"title":"The firing declines","detail":"Artillery and musket fire diminish as the sun sets through battlefield smoke."},{"time":"16:08","audioTime":968,"title":"Combat on September 17 ends","detail":"Darkness closes the deadliest single day in American military history."},{"time":"17:36","audioTime":1056,"title":"The night of the wounded begins","detail":"Stretcher-bearers and medical personnel search the fields and carry casualties to improvised hospitals."},{"time":"19:17","audioTime":1157,"title":"Casualty estimates are presented","detail":"The episode gives category totals and emphasizes that exact Confederate figures remain uncertain."},{"time":"20:20","audioTime":1220,"title":"Letterman’s ambulance corps is assessed","detail":"The new organized system assigns ambulances, drivers, and trained attendants to remove wounded soldiers."},{"time":"20:59","audioTime":1259,"title":"Dozens of field hospitals receive casualties","detail":"The episode counts at least 71 Federal hospitals in buildings around the battlefield."},{"time":"22:00","audioTime":1320,"title":"Clara Barton brings supplies","detail":"Barton’s wagons deliver urgently needed medical material to the northern battlefield."},{"time":"22:11","audioTime":1331,"title":"The Sanitary Commission sends relief","detail":"The civilian organization uses its own transportation to bring additional supplies the day after the battle."},{"time":"22:24","audioTime":1344,"title":"Official medical stores remain delayed","detail":"The episode places the arrival of substantial Army Medical Department supplies on the evening of September 20."},{"time":"22:39","audioTime":1359,"title":"Evacuation to Frederick begins","detail":"Walking wounded and ambulances travel about twenty miles through Middletown to the larger hospital center."},{"time":"23:03","audioTime":1383,"title":"Most movable wounded leave the battlefield","detail":"By September 25, about 600 severely wounded soldiers remain in two tent hospitals near Sharpsburg."},{"time":"23:25","audioTime":1405,"title":"Frederick expands hospital capacity","detail":"Doctors add six temporary hospitals in churches and other buildings to the existing military barracks."},{"time":"24:27","audioTime":1467,"title":"Lee leaves wounded soldiers behind","detail":"The Confederate withdrawal cannot carry all casualties, and about 2,000 remain in Federal hands in the episode’s estimate."},{"time":"25:36","audioTime":1536,"title":"Confederate battlefield strength is discussed","detail":"The episode compares Lee’s estimate of no more than 35,000 engaged with Ezra Carman’s estimate of just over 38,000 effectives."},{"time":"26:10","audioTime":1570,"title":"Federal losses concentrate in four corps","detail":"The episode states that about 96 percent of Federal casualties occurred in I, II, XII, and IX Corps."},{"time":"26:44","audioTime":1604,"title":"A third of the Federal army did not fire","detail":"Large parts of V and VI Corps remained uncommitted or saw only limited action."},{"time":"26:53","audioTime":1613,"title":"Lee decides to remain","detail":"That evening he orders the army reorganized and provisioned without issuing a retreat order."},{"time":"27:22","audioTime":1642,"title":"McClellan predicts renewed fighting","detail":"On the morning of September 18 he tells Halleck and his wife that the battle will probably resume."},{"time":"28:11","audioTime":1691,"title":"Franklin proposes another assault","detail":"The VI Corps commander again recommends attacking the Confederate left, but McClellan declines."},{"time":"28:31","audioTime":1711,"title":"Lee withdraws after dark","detail":"A truce permits collection of wounded and burial of dead; the Army of Northern Virginia then crosses the Potomac."},{"time":"28:44","audioTime":1724,"title":"Federal pursuit reaches the river","detail":"V Corps troops cross on September 19, and a larger force is repulsed by Hill at Shepherdstown on September 20."},{"time":"29:13","audioTime":1753,"title":"The battle’s result is assessed","detail":"The episode distinguishes the tactical standstill from the strategic failure of Lee’s Maryland invasion."},{"time":"34:50","audioTime":2090,"title":"Antietam becomes a decisive event","detail":"Lee’s withdrawal ends the invasion even though the Army of Northern Virginia is not destroyed."},{"time":"35:01","audioTime":2101,"title":"Lincoln acts after the battle","detail":"The Union strategic victory supplies the occasion for announcing the Preliminary Emancipation Proclamation on September 22."},{"time":"35:52","audioTime":2152,"title":"The book recommendations begin","detail":"The hosts recommend two Antietam essay collections edited by Gary W. Gallagher."},{"time":"39:36","audioTime":2376,"title":"A Canadian connection is added","detail":"The bonus segment introduces Calixa Lavallée, a Quebec-born musician who served in the 4th Rhode Island."},{"time":"40:19","audioTime":2419,"title":"Lavallée is wounded at Antietam","detail":"The future composer of the music for “O Canada” is wounded when Hill’s counterattack hits the regiment."}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"#315f8c","branches":[{"commander":"Maj. Gen. George B. McClellan","command":"Army of the Potomac","subordinates":"Retained V Corps as a reserve, sent artillery but no infantry to Burnside, declined Franklin’s September 18 proposal, and did not prevent Lee’s withdrawal"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Fitz John Porter","command":"V Corps","subordinates":"Kept most of his infantry near the center as the army reserve; elements crossed the Potomac during the September 19–20 pursuit"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Ambrose E. Burnside","command":"Ninth Corps and Army of the Potomac left wing","subordinates":"Requested reinforcements while the corps held its bridgehead west of Antietam Creek"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. Jacob D. Cox","command":"Immediate command of the Ninth Corps","subordinates":"Ordered the advance near Sharpsburg withdrawn and formed a defensive line above the Lower Bridge"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. Isaac P. Rodman","command":"3rd Division, Ninth Corps","subordinates":"Tried to change front against Hill’s arrival and was mortally wounded while riding toward the threatened flank"},{"commander":"Col. Edward Harland","command":"2nd Brigade, Rodman’s division","subordinates":"Commanded the 8th and 16th Connecticut and 4th Rhode Island; attempted to restore the separated brigade after his horse was shot"},{"commander":"Col. Eliakim P. Scammon","command":"Kanawha Division","subordinates":"Used the 12th, 23rd, and 30th Ohio to change front and oppose Hill’s counterattack before withdrawing into Cox’s final line"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. George G. Meade","command":"I Corps after Hooker was wounded","subordinates":"Controlled the northern Federal sector where concentrated artillery made Jackson’s proposed flank movement impracticable"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. William B. Franklin","command":"VI Corps","subordinates":"Proposed another attack against the Confederate left on September 18; McClellan rejected it"},{"commander":"Maj. Jonathan Letterman","command":"Medical director, Army of the Potomac","subordinates":"Directed the new ambulance system and the movement of wounded men from the field to hospitals"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"#963b31","branches":[{"commander":"Gen. Robert E. Lee","command":"Army of Northern Virginia","subordinates":"Reinforced the threatened right, remained in position through September 18, and withdrew across the Potomac after dark"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Thomas J. “Stonewall” Jackson","command":"Confederate left wing","subordinates":"Assessed a movement around the Federal right but abandoned it because Federal artillery and the Potomac closed the route"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. J. E. B. Stuart","command":"Cavalry and mixed force on the Confederate left","subordinates":"Prepared the attempted flank movement and canceled it after Federal artillery suppressed his batteries"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. A. P. Hill","command":"Light Division","subordinates":"Marched from Harpers Ferry and directed the counterattack that broke the Ninth Corps left"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. Maxcy Gregg","command":"South Carolina brigade, Light Division","subordinates":"Attacked the 16th Connecticut and 4th Rhode Island in Otto’s 40-acre cornfield"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. James J. Archer","command":"Tennessee, Alabama, and Georgia brigade, Light Division","subordinates":"Advanced between Gregg and Branch against the Ninth Corps left and its Ohio supports"},{"commander":"Brig. Gen. Lawrence O’Bryan Branch","command":"North Carolina brigade, Light Division","subordinates":"Drove north toward the 8th Connecticut and Fairchild’s brigade and was killed during the attack"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. David R. Jones","command":"Division on the Confederate right","subordinates":"Combined his surviving infantry and artillery with Hill’s arriving force south of Sharpsburg"}]}],"people":[{"name":"Isaac P. Rodman","role":"Ninth Corps division commander who identified Hill’s approach, tried to reorient the Federal left, and was mortally wounded","side":"United States","image":"/images/ep199-isaac-rodman.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2023631962/"},{"name":"Jacob D. Cox","role":"Immediate Ninth Corps commander who withdrew the exposed advance and held the Lower Bridge bridgehead","side":"United States","source":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=236"},{"name":"Edward Harland","role":"Brigade commander whose separated Connecticut and Rhode Island regiments received Hill’s attack","side":"United States","source":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=316"},{"name":"George G. Meade","role":"I Corps commander in the northern sector where Federal artillery stopped the proposed Confederate flank movement","side":"United States","source":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=25"},{"name":"George B. McClellan","role":"Army of the Potomac commander who retained V Corps, declined to renew the battle, and allowed Lee to withdraw","side":"United States","source":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=19"},{"name":"Fitz John Porter","role":"V Corps commander whose largely fresh infantry remained the army reserve during the final crisis","side":"United States","source":"https://www.nps.gov/people/george-brinton-mcclellan.htm"},{"name":"Jonathan Letterman","role":"Army medical director responsible for the organized ambulance and evacuation system tested at Antietam","side":"United States","source":"https://home.nps.gov/articles/000/jonathan-letterman.htm"},{"name":"Clara Barton","role":"Independent relief worker who brought medical supplies, food, water, and lanterns to the battlefield","side":"United States","source":"https://www.nps.gov/anti/learn/historyculture/clarabarton.htm"},{"name":"Calixa Lavallée","role":"4th Rhode Island musician wounded in Hill’s attack who later composed the music for “O Canada”","side":"United States","source":"https://www.canada.ca/en/canadian-heritage/services/anthem-canada/history-o-canada.html"},{"name":"A. P. Hill","role":"Light Division commander whose arrival from Harpers Ferry stopped the Ninth Corps advance","side":"Confederacy","source":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=57"},{"name":"Lawrence O’Bryan Branch","role":"North Carolina brigade commander killed while leading the final Confederate counterattack","side":"Confederacy","image":"/images/ep199-lawrence-branch.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2010649184/"},{"name":"Maxcy Gregg","role":"South Carolina brigade commander whose troops struck the 16th Connecticut and 4th Rhode Island","side":"Confederacy","source":"https://www.nps.gov/anti/planyourvisit/final-attack-trail-stop-7.htm"},{"name":"Thomas J. “Stonewall” Jackson","role":"Confederate wing commander who assessed and abandoned a movement around the Federal right","side":"Confederacy","source":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=41"},{"name":"J. E. B. Stuart","role":"Cavalry commander assigned the aborted northern flank movement","side":"Confederacy","source":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=58"},{"name":"Robert E. Lee","role":"Army of Northern Virginia commander who held on September 18 and withdrew across the Potomac that night","side":"Confederacy","source":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=15"}],"losses":[{"name":"Maj. Gen. Joseph K. F. Mansfield","side":"United States","role":"XII Corps commander","outcome":"Mortally wounded in the chest near the East Woods during the morning fighting and died on September 18, 1862."},{"name":"Maj. Gen. Israel B. Richardson","side":"United States","role":"1st Division, II Corps commander","outcome":"Mortally wounded by a shell fragment after the Sunken Road breakthrough and died on November 3, 1862."},{"name":"Brig. Gen. Isaac P. Rodman","side":"United States","role":"3rd Division, Ninth Corps commander","outcome":"Mortally wounded through the chest while changing front against Hill’s counterattack and died on September 30, 1862."},{"name":"Brig. Gen. William E. Starke","side":"Confederacy","role":"Stonewall Division commander","outcome":"Wounded three times while leading a morning counterattack and died within the hour."},{"name":"Brig. Gen. George B. Anderson","side":"Confederacy","role":"North Carolina brigade commander in the Sunken Road","outcome":"Mortally wounded in the foot, underwent amputation after infection, and died on October 16, 1862."},{"name":"Brig. Gen. Lawrence O’Bryan Branch","side":"Confederacy","role":"North Carolina brigade commander, Light Division","outcome":"Shot in the head and killed while leading his brigade in A. P. Hill’s late-afternoon counterattack."}],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/ep199-hill-counterattack.svg","alt":"Schematic tactical map of A. P. Hill’s counterattack against the Ninth Corps left.","caption":"Hill’s three engaged brigades struck the separated Federal flank and forced Cox to abandon the advance near Sharpsburg.","source":"Antietam National Battlefield, official reports, Antietam Battlefield Board atlas, and episode transcript."},{"image":"/images/ep199-close-and-aftermath.svg","alt":"Schematic context map of the close of fighting, Confederate withdrawal, and Federal medical evacuation route.","caption":"The armies remained near Sharpsburg on September 18; Lee withdrew that night while the evacuation of wounded soldiers expanded east toward Frederick.","source":"Antietam National Battlefield timeline and medical interpretation, official reports, and episode transcript."},{"image":"/images/ep199-isaac-rodman.jpg","alt":"Portrait of United States brigadier general Isaac Peace Rodman in uniform.","caption":"Rodman was mortally wounded while attempting to reorient his division against Hill’s attack.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division."},{"image":"/images/ep199-lawrence-branch.jpg","alt":"Portrait of Lawrence O’Bryan Branch before the Civil War.","caption":"Branch was killed while leading his North Carolina brigade in the final Confederate counterattack.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division."},{"image":"/images/ep199-smith-hospital-barn.jpg","alt":"Alexander Gardner photograph of the Smith house and hospital barn near Keedysville.","caption":"The Smith property was one of the many improvised hospitals used after Antietam.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division."}],"sources":[{"label":"Official Civil War Podcast episode 199 page","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/2017/06/11/civil-war-podcast-episode-199/"},{"label":"Antietam National Battlefield: Final Attack overview","url":"https://www.nps.gov/places/the-final-attack.htm"},{"label":"Antietam National Battlefield: Final Attack Trail stop 5, Caught in the Corn","url":"https://www.nps.gov/anti/planyourvisit/final-attack-trail-stop-5.htm"},{"label":"Antietam National Battlefield: Final Attack Trail stop 7","url":"https://www.nps.gov/anti/planyourvisit/final-attack-trail-stop-7.htm"},{"label":"Antietam National Battlefield: Otto Farm","url":"https://www.nps.gov/anti/learn/historyculture/the-otto-farm.htm"},{"label":"Antietam National Battlefield: casualty estimates","url":"https://www.nps.gov/anti/learn/historyculture/casualties.htm"},{"label":"Antietam National Battlefield: six generals killed or mortally wounded","url":"https://www.nps.gov/anti/learn/historyculture/6generals.htm"},{"label":"Antietam National Battlefield: Maryland Campaign timeline, part 2","url":"https://www.nps.gov/anti/learn/historyculture/timeline2.htm"},{"label":"Antietam National Battlefield: lead-up and aftermath","url":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/antietam-lead-up-to-and-after.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: medicine and medical practices","url":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/medicine-and-medical-practices.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Jonathan Letterman","url":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/jonathan-letterman.htm"},{"label":"Antietam National Battlefield: Clara Barton at Antietam","url":"https://www.nps.gov/anti/learn/historyculture/clarabarton.htm"},{"label":"Antietam National Battlefield: Joseph Poffenberger farm","url":"https://www.nps.gov/anti/learn/historyculture/joseph-poffenberger-farm.htm"},{"label":"Primary source: A. P. Hill’s official report","url":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=57"},{"label":"Primary source: Jacob D. Cox’s official report","url":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=236"},{"label":"Primary source: Edward Harland’s official report","url":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=316"},{"label":"Primary source: Thomas J. Jackson’s official report","url":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=41"},{"label":"Primary source: J. E. B. Stuart’s official report","url":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=58"},{"label":"Primary source: George G. Meade’s official report","url":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=25"},{"label":"Primary source: Robert E. Lee’s official report","url":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=15"},{"label":"Primary source: George B. McClellan’s official reports","url":"https://antietam.aotw.org/exhibit.php?exhibit_id=19"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Antietam battlefield atlas","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/map05000006/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Isaac Peace Rodman portrait","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2023631962/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Lawrence O’Bryan Branch portrait","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2010649184/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Smith barn hospital photograph","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018671855/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Preliminary Emancipation Proclamation context","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/prn-12-223/"},{"label":"Government of Canada: history of “O Canada”","url":"https://www.canada.ca/en/canadian-heritage/services/anthem-canada/history-o-canada.html"},{"label":"Official podcast book-recommendations list","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/book-recommendations/"}]}}]}]
